# November 19, 2026: southwestern Otsego County, New York, with Sidney over the line in Delaware County: Unadilla,
# Sidney, Gilbertsville (Butternuts), Morris, Otsdawa (Otego), Oneonta and the Colliersville dam. Drawn at random by
# tools/choose.py --state ny (region cat), which offered Windsor, Tannersville and Unadilla; the Atlas had seven places
# within 20 km of Unadilla and only the roundhouse lead in Oneonta. Places in the Unadilla and Sidney valley take
# reg 'cat' as the draw said (their nearest Atlas places are cat); the ones farther north in Otsego County take 'scho',
# the region the Oneonta roundhouse and Rockwells Mills already use.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py, including the Otsego County Register list),
# HMdb markers and the William G. Pomeroy Foundation's marker pages, the Episcopal Diocese of Albany's parish pages,
# themajorsinn.com, This Is Cooperstown's Gilbertsville guide, the Swart-Wilcox House's own site and Hanford Mills'
# listing of it, Huntington Memorial Library's site, All Otsego news articles, WZOZ and CNY News on the Oneonta Theatre,
# the Friends of the Oneonta Theatre history page, and Michigan Tech's industrial archaeology archive. Every Register
# form tried for this ground (92001079, 92001080, 11000290, 97001455, 97001456, 02000143) is an NPGallery placeholder.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HM(m,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+m]

BATCH={'key':'cat_qhid','label':'Southwestern Otsego County, NY: Unadilla, Gilbertsville, Morris and Oneonta','added':'2026-11-19 10:42'}

PLACES=[
N('unadilla-village-district','Unadilla village: the Catskill Turnpike crossing and the limestone underpass','Unadilla','cat','town','standing',42.32528,-75.31111,'area','public','walk','','1770s–1940s',
  'Unadilla began at Wattles Ferry, where the Catskill Turnpike crossed the Susquehanna at the east end of the present village; the settlement at the mouth of the Ouleout was burned by the Continental Army in 1778 with its neighbour across the river and settled again afterwards. The turnpike made it a stop on the road west until the Albany and Susquehanna Railroad (later the Delaware and Hudson) took the traffic. The Register district listed in 1992 holds 145 contributing buildings, four churches, eleven commercial buildings, a historic cemetery, three monuments and a limestone railroad underpass, in Georgian and Federal as well as later styles.',
  'Walk the main street for the Georgian and Federal houses and the four churches. St. Matthew\'s Episcopal parish, organized in 1809, calls itself the village\'s first congregation and first house of worship (the building\'s own date was not found). The Roswell Wright House (1823) is a temple-fronted Greek Revival house with four Ionic columns (private; view from the street). Look for the limestone underpass that carries the railroad. Exact positions of the underpass and the cemetery were not checked; interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'A village of public streets; the houses are private. Street parking in the village (not checked). St. Matthew\'s holds services on Sundays (times not checked).',
  [W('Unadilla Village Historic District'),W('Unadilla (village), New York'),W('Roswell Wright House'),L('Diocese of Albany: St. Matthew\'s, Unadilla','https://albanyepiscopaldiocese.org/st-matthews-church-unadilla-ny/'),HM('92999','Routes of the Armies of Sullivan and Clinton')],brick='?'),
N('andrew-mann-inn','Andrew Mann Inn','Unadilla','cat','hotel','standing',42.32099,-75.39077,'exact','view','drive','','1787',
  'Built in 1787 as the home of David Baits and made an inn in 1795, it served stagecoach travellers on the Catskill Turnpike until about 1860, as the marker in front says. A two-story, five-bay main block with a two-story wing, heavy timber framing and clapboards, it is a well-kept example of the change from late Georgian to Federal. Listed on the National Register in 1980.',
  'The five-bay clapboarded front on State Route 7, with the HMdb-listed roadside marker. Interior not checked.',
  'A turnpike tavern from the 1790s still on its road.',
  'Private (383 State Highway 7, Town of Unadilla, Sidney post office). View from the road only; owner not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Andrew Mann Inn'),HM('92887','Andrew Mann Inn')],brick='no'),
N('old-unadilla-marker-sidney','Old Unadilla: the town burned in 1778','Sidney','cat','town','marker',42.31628,-75.40201,'site','public','drive','','1778',
  'Old Unadilla, where the Unadilla meets the Susquehanna, was a settlement of white colonists until 1777, when Joseph Brant arrived and demanded supplies; many settlers left and Brant\'s Mohawk followers and Loyalists stayed, using the town as a base for raids such as the attack on German Flatts in September 1778. On October 10 and 11, 1778 the Continental Army under Colonel William Butler burned it, before the Sullivan-Clinton campaign of the next year. Sidney stands on the site.',
  'A blue-and-gold Pomeroy Foundation marker (2016) on Route 7. Nothing of the town is said to be visible.',
  '',
  'Roadside marker (HMdb address 294 Route 7; the Pomeroy page gives 240 State Highway 7, Sidney). The nearest mapped lot is Keith Clark Park on River Street, about 430 m south-east.',
  [HM('98167','Old Unadilla'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Old Unadilla','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/old-unadilla')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('sidney-covered-bridge-abutment','Sidney covered bridge: the replica and the old abutment','Sidney','cat','landmark','traces',42.31479,-75.40343,'area','view','walk','','1852–1894',
  'A Burr arch-kingpost covered bridge was built across the Susquehanna at the end of Bridge Street in 1852, the first toll bridge and the first bridge of any kind across the river at Sidney: 25 cents for a team and carriage, 8 for a horse and cart, 2 for someone on foot. An iron bridge replaced it in 1894, and the Main Street bridge took the crossing in 1936. The marker says the covered bridge\'s stone abutment can still be seen at the end of Bridge Street.',
  'The stone abutment at the river end of Bridge Street (the marker\'s words). A replica of the covered bridge, built by Unadilla Laminated Products, stands in Sidney Firemen\'s Memorial Park off River Street with the marker. The pin is the replica and marker; the abutment, at the end of Bridge Street about 1 km east, was not found on the aerial.',
  '',
  'Village street; the river bank beside the abutment was not checked for ownership, so look from the street. The marker and replica are in Sidney Firemen\'s Memorial Park off River Street, with the Keith Clark Park lot on River Street nearby.',
  [HM('220631','The Covered Bridge at Sidney')],brick='no',th=[42.31278,-75.39971],thn='Keith Clark Park lot, River Street (the replica and marker are nearby)'),
N('majors-inn-gilbert-block','Major\'s Inn and the Gilbert Block, Gilbertsville','Gilbertsville','scho','hotel','standing',42.47134,-75.32708,'exact','view','walk','','1893–1917',
  'Gilbertsville\'s centre is two Tudor-style buildings of the Gilbert family. The Gilbert Block, three attached two-story brick commercial buildings of 1893–1895 by the Boston architect Henry Forbes Bigelow, holds shops and studios. Major\'s Inn, begun in 1896 for Major James L. Gilbert on the site of the 1822 Gilbert homestead that burned in 1895 and finished in 1917, is a rambling slate-roofed building with towers, balconies, bays and porches; quatrefoils decorate both. After changing hands through the 1950s to the 1970s, the inn was bought by the Major\'s Inn Foundation, which has restored much of it and runs it as a cultural centre and event venue. The whole village is a Register district (1974, enlarged 1982).',
  'The towers, conical roofs and porches of the inn (a 2006 Wikimedia photograph shows stone walls below half-timbering and slate, with red brick chimneys), and the brick Gilbert Block beside it. The foundation holds community events through the year (a quilt show among them) and rents the building; the interior is seen at those. Interior fabric not checked. The village round it is white clapboard houses, churches and the Empire House (about 1835).',
  'A Tudor manor in a valley of white farmhouses.',
  'Owned by the Major\'s Inn Foundation, 12 Marion Avenue (State Route 51). No regular opening hours were found; see the foundation\'s events. The shops in the Gilbert Block keep their own hours. Street parking (not checked).',
  [W('Major\'s Inn and Gilbert Block'),W('Gilbertsville Historic District'),L('The Major\'s Inn','https://themajorsinn.com'),L('This Is Cooperstown: Gilbertsville day trip','https://www.thisiscooperstown.com/blog/post/gilbertsville-day-trip-guide/'),HM('169661','Gilbertsville, NY')],brick='some'),
N('gilbertsville-old-village-cemetery','Old Village Cemetery, Gilbertsville','Gilbertsville','scho','burial','standing',42.47454,-75.3245,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','',
  'The old burying ground on the hill above Gilbertsville holds Revolutionary War veterans, named on a memorial the town and village put up for the 250th year in 2026: Alexander Bryant, William Burgess, David Goff, Samuel Leonard, John Thorp and Jeremiah Townsend. Its founding date was not found.',
  'The 2026 memorial, and stone steps up from Cemetery Road into the cemetery (HMdb).',
  '',
  'On Cemetery Road near Marion Avenue, a short walk from the village centre; owner and hours not checked. No mapped lot; park in the village.',
  [HM('307247','Old Village Cemetery')],brick='no'),
N('zion-episcopal-morris','Zion Episcopal Church and Harmony Cemetery, Morris','Morris','scho','worship','standing',42.54885,-75.24276,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1818',
  'Zion Church grew from services in settlers\' homes in the Butternut Valley; the Rev. Daniel Nash founded it, and the stone church was built in 1818 on land given by General Jacob Morris. It is in an early English Gothic Revival style, with a steep roof and a central bell tower with a balustraded belfry. The outside is unchanged; the interior was renovated and enlarged with a chancel in 1868. The rectory (1893), parish house (1901) and Harmony Cemetery, with burials from about 1800 to 1937, are in the same Register district (1997).',
  'The 1818 stone church and tower, and the old cemetery. Interior seen at services; its fabric not checked.',
  'One of the oldest Gothic Revival churches in upstate New York, in stone.',
  'An active Episcopal parish of the Diocese of Albany. Services on Sundays (time not checked); on the last Sunday of June, July and August the service moves to All Saints Chapel. Street parking in Morris (not checked).',
  [W('Zion Episcopal Church Complex and Harmony Cemetery'),L('Diocese of Albany: Zion Church, Morris','https://albanyepiscopaldiocese.org/zion-church-morris-ny/'),HM('144269','Morris Village Historic District')],brick='no'),
N('all-saints-chapel-morris','All Saints Chapel and the Morris family burial ground','Morris','scho','worship','standing',42.50919,-75.28817,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1791–1868',
  'A small stone Gothic Revival chapel built about 1866 to 1868 on the Morris family estate, three bays wide and four deep, with a slate roof, a bell-cote and a large rose window. The family burial ground beside it has its first burial in 1791 and was still in use in 2025. A wagon shed of the 1860s also stands. Listed on the National Register in 1997.',
  'The stone chapel with its rose window and bell-cote, the family graves and the wagon shed. Interior seen only at the summer services; its fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'On State Route 51 south of Morris, on what was the Morris estate; the grounds are not described as open. The Diocese of Albany lists services at 10 a.m. on the last Sunday of June, July and August, the only times found when it is open. Otherwise view from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('All Saints Chapel and Morris Family Burial Ground'),L('Diocese of Albany: All Saints Chapel, Morris Manor','https://albanyepiscopaldiocese.org/all-saints-chapel-morris-manor-ny-2/')],brick='no'),
N('otsdawa-baptist-church','Otsdawa Baptist Church','Otego','scho','worship','standing',42.48630,-75.17355,'site','view','drive','','1840',
  'A Free Will Baptist church built in 1840 in the hamlet of Otsdawa: one story, three bays by three, wood frame with clapboards on a stone foundation, Greek Revival, with a two-stage tower and small spire. Listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'The Greek Revival front and tower. Interior not checked; whether it still holds services was not checked.',
  '',
  'On County Road 8 in Otsdawa. Owner and use not checked: view from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Otsdawa Baptist Church')],brick='no'),
N('swart-wilcox-house','Swart-Wilcox House Museum','Oneonta','scho','landmark','standing',42.44509,-75.06997,'site','public','walk','','1807',
  'Lawrence Swart (1753–1841), a Revolutionary War soldier, built this German Palatine vernacular farmhouse on the bank of the Susquehanna in 1807 on 230 acres bought from the Wallace Patent; it is the oldest house in Oneonta and was home to only two families in over 200 years. The city bought the decaying house in 1972 and it is now a community house museum, chartered by the state. Listed on the National Register in 1990.',
  'A one-and-a-half-story frame house with clapboards and a gable roof, with an attached shed and carriage shed. Open for informal tours inside after the summer Sunday programs on the front lawn; interior fabric not checked.',
  'The oldest house in Oneonta, in a 1970s neighbourhood.',
  'City-owned museum on Wilcox Avenue near Henry Street. Its own site: open every Sunday in summer for informal tours and the Summer Sunday Series (bring a lawn chair), no charge, accessible; group tours on request. Exact times not checked. The nearest mapped lot is on London Avenue, about 70 m away (not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Swart-Wilcox House'),L('Swart-Wilcox House Museum','https://swartwilcoxhouse.wordpress.com'),L('Hanford Mills: Swart-Wilcox House Museum','https://exploremore.hanfordmills.org/swart-wilcox-house-museum/'),HM('169666','Swart-Wilcox House')],brick='no'),
N('oneonta-dh-station','Delaware & Hudson passenger station, Oneonta','Oneonta','scho','rail','standing',42.45257,-75.05968,'exact','view','walk','stations','1892–1963',
  'The Delaware and Hudson built this brick passenger station in 1892, when Oneonta was a railroad town with what was called the largest roundhouse in the world. Passenger service ended in January 1963. The building became offices and then, for many years, a restaurant (latterly Stella Luna); the Greater Oneonta Historical Society put a Pomeroy marker in its parking lot in July 2024.',
  'The brick station building on Market Street with its marker. Current use not checked (All Otsego in 2024 called it the former Stella Luna restaurant); interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private building at 58–60 Market Street. View from Market Street; parking not checked.',
  [HM('316004','D&H Station'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: D&H Station','https://wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/dh-station'),L('All Otsego: marker unveiled','https://www.allotsego.com/the-ties-that-bind-historical-association-unveils-dh-marker/')],brick='?'),
N('oneonta-armory','Oneonta Armory','Oneonta','scho','civic','standing',42.45095,-75.06535,'exact','view','walk','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 for the National Guard to a design by State Architect George L. Heins, a brick and stone castle-like building: a two-story administration block with a five-story octagonal tower at the south-west corner and a square tower at the north-west, and a drill shed behind. It was home to Company G of the 1st Regiment. The city acquired it in 2003 as the state sold off armories; it is now the Asa C. Allison Jr. Municipal Building, with recreation programs in the gym and a teen centre in the basement, and the city has asked residents what else it should hold.',
  'The octagonal corner tower (rock-faced stone at its base, red brick above, in a 2012 Wikimedia photograph) and the drill shed. Interior seen by those using the city programs; fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'City-owned, 2 Academy Street at Fairview Street. Open for city recreation and teen programs (hours not checked); otherwise view from the street. A mapped lot is on Fairview Street.',
  [W('Oneonta Armory'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Oneonta Armory','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/oneonta-armory/'),L('All Otsego: Armory\'s future','https://www.allotsego.com/armorys-future-tbd-by-oneontans')],brick='yes'),
N('oneonta-theatre','Oneonta Theatre','Oneonta','scho','civic','standing',42.4533,-75.06388,'exact','view','walk','','1897',
  'Designed in 1897 by a Rochester architect and opened in 1898, the theatre was one of the first fireproof theatres in the country and an early stop on the vaudeville circuit; a few years after opening the stage house was torn down for a larger stage with a 65-foot fly tower and nine dressing rooms. It was enlarged in 1922, when the entrance moved to the centre. In the 1970s owner Harold DeGraw kept it open through urban renewal by modernising the front and lobby, and in the 1980s he closed off the balcony as a 200-seat cinema. Friends of the Oneonta Theatre formed in 2008; a later owner ran it as a music venue and then closed it, and the Friends are working to restore it.',
  'The three-story red brick block on Chestnut Street (2020 Wikimedia photograph) with storefronts and apartments; the auditorium wing behind runs at 45 degrees. The Friends\' history page says the painted and gilded decoration and murals are probably under later paint. Closed; interior not open as far as checked.',
  'One of the oldest theatres left in New York, waiting to come back.',
  'Privately owned and closed as of the articles read (dates of the latest not settled). View from Chestnut Street.',
  [W('Oneonta Theatre'),L('Friends of the Oneonta Theatre: history','https://sites.google.com/view/47chestnut/history'),L('WZOZ: inside the Oneonta Theatre','https://wzozfm.com/exclusive-look-at-progress-inside-oneonta-new-yorks-vibrant-theatre/'),L('CNY News: what\'s next for the theatre','https://cnynews.com/whats-next-for-the-closed-down-historic-oneonta-theatre/')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('fairchild-mansion-oneonta','Fairchild Mansion','Oneonta','scho','landmark','standing',42.45603,-75.05877,'exact','view','walk','','1867',
  'A three-story Queen Anne brick house with a turret, gables, a pedimented entrance porch and a porte-cochère. Built in 1867, it was enlarged and brought up to date in 1897 and 1915 by George W. Fairchild (1845–1924). The Oneonta Masonic Lodge took it over in 1929. Listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The red brick walls, the turret and the roof of Conosera tiles made by the Celadon Terra Cotta Company. Whether the lodge still uses it, and the interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'Private, 318 Main Street (OpenStreetMap). View from the street.',
  [W('Fairchild Mansion')],brick='yes'),
N('huntington-memorial-library','Huntington Memorial Library (the Huntington homestead)','Oneonta','scho','civic','standing',42.45407,-75.06436,'exact','public','walk','','1917',
  'Henry E. Huntington, the railroad executive, gave his old family homestead and its land to the city in 1917 as a home for the library and a park, and endowed it with a trust fund; the library opened there in July 1920. The grounds are Huntington Park.',
  'The homestead, now the library, and Huntington Park round it. Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Public library in a building given to the city for it, 62 Chestnut Street. Its site lists Saturday 9 to 4 and Tuesday to Thursday 9 to 8 (the full week not checked). Huntington Park has parking along Dietz Street (All Otsego).',
  [L('Huntington Memorial Library','https://hmloneonta.org'),HM('259282','Huntington Home')],brick='?'),
N('wilber-house-oneonta','George I. Wilber House (CANO arts center)','Oneonta','scho','landmark','standing',42.45578,-75.06183,'exact','view','walk','','1875–1890',
  'George I. Wilber (1845–1922), president of the Wilber National Bank from 1890, built this Queen Anne house in two stages, 1875 and about 1890: three stories of wood frame on a stone foundation, with a round three-story corner tower, cross gables and a large decorated wrap-around porch with a porte-cochère. Since 1997 it has housed the Upper Catskill Community Council of the Arts, now the Community Arts Network of Oneonta. Listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'The round tower and the porch. Interior seen during the arts center\'s shows; its fabric and hours not checked.',
  '',
  'Arts center at 11 Ford Avenue; hours not checked. View from Ford Avenue.',
  [W('George I. Wilber House'),HM('302784','Wilber Mansion')],brick='no'),
N('oneonta-old-post-office','Old Post Office (Oneonta City Hall)','Oneonta','scho','civic','standing',42.45472,-75.06056,'site','public','walk','','1915',
  'One of the post offices the Treasury\'s Supervising Architect\'s office (Oscar Wenderoth) designed in New York State, built in 1915: a near-square block of seven bays a side in Indiana limestone with Concord granite trim, and a giant portico of six Corinthian columns. In 1980 the city moved its offices here from the 1906 Municipal Building (Old City Hall) on Main Street.',
  'The limestone portico. The Old City Hall of 1906 (painted brick and terra cotta, with a 1978 clock tower) is nearby on Main Street. Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Oneonta city offices (Wikipedia): open to the public in business hours (not checked). Street and municipal parking downtown (not checked).',
  [W('Old Post Office (Oneonta, New York)'),W('Municipal Building (Oneonta, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('colliersville-dam','Colliersville Dam (Goodyear Lake)','Milford','scho','water','standing',42.50364,-74.98463,'site','view','drive','','1906–1907',
  'The dam across the Susquehanna at Colliersville made Goodyear Lake, flooding 600 acres, and supplied hydroelectric power to the Otsego & Mohawk trolley line and nearby villages. The roadside marker says it was built in 1907 at a cost of $200,000; Michigan Tech\'s industrial archaeology archive says New York State Electric and Gas\'s dam and hydrostation was built in 1906 by the Stevens-Hewitt Engineering Company of New York City.',
  'The dam and powerhouse from State Route 28, where the Goodyear Lake Association\'s marker stands. Current owner and whether the plant still runs not checked.',
  '',
  'Utility property: view from Route 28. The nearest mapped lot is the Goodyear Lake waterway access on the east shore, about 800 m away.',
  [W('Goodyear Lake'),HM('98149','Colliersville Dam'),L('Michigan Tech IA archive: Colliersville Dam and Hydrostation','https://indarch.mtu.edu/node/18973')],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=[42.50469,-74.97511],thn='Goodyear Lake waterway access lot'),
]

